  <!-- VICTORIAN PROPERTIES -->
  <section class="pf-section" style="background:#f8fafc;">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Bathroom Fitting in Liverpool's Victorian and Period Properties</h2>
      <p class="pf-sub">Liverpool has one of the highest counts of Victorian and Edwardian housing in England. We have fitted bathrooms across all of it.</p>

      <div class="pf-card pf-card-navy" style="margin-top:2rem;">
        <h3>The challenges of Victorian Liverpool bathrooms</h3>
        <p>Liverpool Victorian terraces often have the bathroom in a position added as a later build. Over an outrigger, in a converted small bedroom or in a ground-floor extension. The waste pipes in these spots need longer runs to reach the soil stack. Old joists may need brace work to hold the weight of a freestanding bath or a big vanity.</p>
        <p>Solid brick walls in Liverpool Victorian housing will not take chased-in pipework as a stud wall does. Supply pipes are either boxed in neatly or routed under the floor. Old cast iron waste pipes may need part-replacement where they have rusted out. We spot all of this at the free survey. Any remedial work is in the fixed-price quote before any job starts.</p>
        <p>Some period rooms have features the owner wants to keep. We plan the fit to protect the old cornice, picture rails and skirting boards. We can also restore or fit copies of features that earlier work damaged.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="pf-card pf-card-orange" style="margin-top:2rem;">
        <h3>What works in a Liverpool Victorian bathroom</h3>
        <p>Both period and modern styles work in Victorian rooms. The period look is a roll-top bath from BC Designs, a high-cistern toilet, a column basin, metro or hex tiles and brushed brass taps. The modern look is clean fittings in a room that keeps its old cornice work. Mixed metals give depth in older rooms.</p>
        <p>Underfloor heating is almost always our advice in a Victorian property. The thick brick walls keep the room cold without it. The extra cost is small. The comfort change is huge. Pair it with a heated towel rail for a bathroom that feels warm from the moment you walk in.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

